# The nightly backup, run by waku-backup.timer.
#
# @WAKU_BACKUP@ IS A PLACEHOLDER AND MUST STAY ONE, for the reason
# waku-firewall.service carries in capitals: a hardcoded
# /srv/waku/src/hosted/deploy/backup.sh installs cleanly on a VM whose checkout
# is somewhere else and then fails at 03:17, in a timer unit, where nobody is
# looking. install.sh substitutes its own directory's backup.sh -- the file it
# is installing this unit beside. A hardcoded path here is a red test in
# evals/deterministic/hosted/test_deploy_scripts.py.
#
# NO Restart= AND NO RemainAfterExit=. A backup that failed tonight is a
# backup to look at, not one to run again in ten seconds against a staging
# slot it may itself have wedged; the timer brings the next attempt.
#
# TimeoutStartSec BECAUSE systemd's DEFAULT FOR A ONESHOT IS INFINITY. Without
# it a run that never returns -- an object store that black-holes a connection
# rather than refusing it is the ordinary way -- holds the staging lock for
# ever, and the lock is shared with restore.sh, so the next night's backup and
# every later restore queue behind a unit nothing will ever end. Six hours is
# chosen to sit above a legitimate whole-fleet backup (one tenant at a time, up
# to 1 GB each) and far below the 24 hours until the timer fires again, so it
# can only ever end a run that is stuck.

[Unit]
Description=waku nightly backup
After=docker.service
Requires=docker.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
TimeoutStartSec=6h
ExecStart=@WAKU_BACKUP@ --all
